        "text": "MIT has long been at the forefront of artificial intelligence (AI) research, shaping the field through groundbreaking discoveries, influential faculty, and pioneering institutions that have defined the trajectory of AI development. Since the 1950s, when the term \"artificial intelligence\" was first coined by John McCarthy at Dartmouth, MIT has played a pivotal role in advancing AI from its theoretical origins to its current status as a transformative technology. The university\u2019s contributions span multiple disciplines, including computer science, engineering, cognitive science, and even philosophy, fostering an interdisciplinary approach that remains central to its AI research today. MIT\u2019s early work in AI was spearheaded by luminaries like Marvin Minsky and John McCarthy, who co-founded the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory in 1959, laying the foundation for many key AI concepts, including neural networks, symbolic reasoning, and machine learning. Minsky\u2019s seminal book *Perceptrons*, published in 1969, became a cornerstone in the field, influencing both the development of AI algorithms and the debates surrounding their limitations. His collaboration with Seymour Papert on the Perceptron project highlighted the mathematical constraints of early neural networks, sparking critical discussions that would later inform the resurgence of deep learning. Meanwhile, McCarthy\u2019s work on time-sharing systems and Lisp programming language\u2014developed at MIT in the late 1950s\u2014became essential tools for AI research, enabling the creation of early AI programs and shaping the software infrastructure that would support future advancements. These foundational contributions established MIT as a leader in AI, attracting top talent and fostering an environment where theoretical and practical innovations could thrive side by side.\n\nThe 1960s and 1970s saw MIT\u2019s AI Laboratory become a hub for ambitious projects that pushed the boundaries of what machines could achieve. One of the most notable was the development of the first AI-based robot, Shakey, created in collaboration with the Stanford Research Institute. Shakey demonstrated the integration of perception, reasoning, and action\u2014key components of intelligent systems\u2014and became a symbol of early AI progress. During this period, MIT also made significant strides in natural language processing with the creation of ELIZA, an early chatbot developed by Joseph Weizenbaum in 1966. Though simple by today\u2019s standards, ELIZA showcased how machines could simulate human conversation, sparking both excitement and ethical concerns about AI\u2019s potential to mimic or manipulate human interactions. These projects, along with work on expert systems and symbolic AI, cemented MIT\u2019s reputation as a place where AI was not just studied but actively built and tested.\n\nIn the 1980s and 1990s, MIT continued to influence AI through its focus on expert systems and symbolic reasoning, while also expanding into new areas like robotics and computer vision. Rodney Brooks, a prominent MIT roboticist, challenged traditional AI paradigms with his \"subsumption architecture,\" which emphasized bottom-up, behavior-based robotics over top-down symbolic systems. His work led to the creation of robots like Cog and Kismet, which were designed to interact with humans in more natural ways, laying the groundwork for modern social robotics and embodied AI. Meanwhile, the MIT Media Lab became a hotbed for AI-driven human-computer interaction, with projects like the \"AI for Everyone\" initiative exploring how AI could enhance creativity, communication, and learning. These decades also saw the rise of machine learning at MIT, with researchers like Patrick Winston and Tomaso Poggio making significant contributions to pattern recognition and learning algorithms, further diversifying the university\u2019s impact on AI.\n\nThe turn of the millennium marked a new era