r Intelligence and the MIT AI Ethics and Governance Project addressing issues like bias, fairness, and the responsible deployment of AI technologies.\n\nMIT\u2019s influence on AI research extends beyond its campus, as its alumni and faculty have founded or led many of the most important AI companies and institutions. John McCarthy, who developed Lisp at MIT, later founded the Stanford AI Laboratory and played a key role in the development of time-sharing systems, which were foundational for modern computing. Marvin Minsky\u2019s students included Ray Kurzweil, who would go on to pioneer speech recognition and text-to-speech technologies, and Danny Hillis, co-founder of Thinking Machines Corporation, which developed some of the earliest parallel supercomputers. More recently, MIT alumni like Demis Hassabis (co-founder of DeepMind), and Andrew Ng (founder of Coursera and former head of AI at Baidu) have led major AI breakthroughs, demonstrating the enduring impact of MIT\u2019s research culture and educational philosophy.\n\nThe university\u2019s research has also shaped AI policy and public discourse, with MIT scholars often at the forefront of debates about the future of AI. In 2014, MIT\u2019s Erik Brynjolfsson and Andrew McAfee published *The Second Machine Age*, which argued that AI and automation were driving a new economic revolution, sparking discussions about the future of work and the need for policy adaptation. Their work on the economics of AI has influenced governments and organizations worldwide, as they grappled with the implications of AI-driven productivity gains and job displacement. Meanwhile, scholars like Iyad Rahwan, director of the MIT Center for Human-Compatible AI, have led efforts to ensure that AI systems are aligned with human values, addressing concerns about AI\u2019s potential to act unpredictably or harmfully.\n\nMIT\u2019s AI research has also had a profound impact on education, both in how AI is taught and in the broader understandin